fact WorkItem(id: "WI-322", description: some(value: "collect_unbound_vars_node (resolve.rs:2720) walks only for_each_child on the Expr arm — Globals living in Apply.type_args / ApplyWithin.type_args / Let.type_annotation (the TermId-typed Expr fields that WI-298 made the opener/closer/substitute walkers handle symmetrically) are NOT detected by the caller-var delay pre-check body_builtins_delay_on_caller_vars_nodes (resolve.rs:2656). PROBLEM: when a builtin delays on a goal whose first positional arg resolves to a var, the pre-check collects unbound vars from that arg and asks 'is any of them a caller var?'. If the arg's structure puts a caller var inside a type_args / type_annotation TermId, the precheck misses it and the rule does NOT propagate delay even though a caller var is genuinely unbound. SYMMETRY: mirror collect_occurrence_global_vars_ordered (node_occurrence.rs ~1188) which already handles this via collect_expr_termid_field_vars (node_occurrence.rs ~1215). SCOPE: extend the Expr arm to also walk Apply.type_args / ApplyWithin.type_args / Let.type_annotation, chasing each TermId via the existing collect_unbound_vars term walker (resolve.rs ~2620). LATENT today because builtin-delay first args are predicate applications without type args in the prelude; live once typer/simp populates type_args at a builtin's first-arg position. Code-review finding on WI-298 PR (98a9ea5). ACCEPTANCE: a delayed builtin whose first arg is f[T = ?caller_var](...) triggers rule delay (caller_var detected through the type_args TermId); cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-17T13:11:36Z"))
